This is an excellent architectural evolution. Moving from a synchronous "Wait and See" model to an asynchronous Event-Driven pattern using Kafka is exactly how enterprise systems handle long-running Airflow tasks.
Here is how we will structure this:
 * The Enum: We add QUEUE("Q") to RequestStatus.
 * The Publisher (Strategy): When the Checker approves the request, we intercept it. Before it saves to the database, we change the status from ACCEPTED to QUEUE and fire the event to Airflow.
 * The Consumer (New Service): We create a brand new service that listens to two distinct topics (one for success, one for failure). It extracts the database ID from the Kafka message, looks up the record, and flips the status to either ACCEPTED or PENDING.
